   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Considering the attached tree in &quot;rooting_issue.png&quot;; in the first panel the tree is unrooted, in the second the tree is rooted on node 14. <div><br></div><div>Why then does the topology collapse to what looks like a polytomy? I don&#39;t think it actually is, as if you zoom in it definitely looks like there are two vertical lines drawn around node 11 in the second panel - is there a way that I have missed to expand the root here? It feels like something I have read but no amount of searching is bringing up something that I can alter...</div><div><br></div><div>I have attached what I think it should look like in &quot;how it should look&quot; png.</div><div><br></div><div>Thanks for any help!</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "wNmgKEJfCAAJ",
   "from": "bena...@gmail.com",
   "author": "65135aac151a06a7",
   "date": "2017-08-15T06:47:24Z",
   "subject": "Re: rooting issue",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Guy,<br><br>Rooting these objects is something that needs to be done very thoughtfully. See <a href=\"http://www.biorxiv.org/content/early/2017/01/13/035360\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.biorxiv.org/content/early/2017/01/13/035360&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792482691029000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2nQ108YGX49GSouWGDkNDW\">http://www.biorxiv.org/content/early/2017/01/13/035360 </a>for what can go wrong if rerooting is done improperly. You didn&#39;t attach any code, so not sure if you are using ggtree::reroot or the new version of ape::root, but don&#39;t use ggtree::reroot if you want to keep node-associated values. Personally if I want to change the rooting, I just get the phylogenetics program to re-output with the topology I want displayed.<br><br>Assuming you are using the newest version of ape::root, then see <a href=\"https://rdrr.io/cran/ape/man/root.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://rdrr.io/cran/ape/man/root.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792482691029000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2HM59vLjJxmJcISWnGWRs9\">https://rdrr.io/cran/ape/man/root.html</a>. In the examples section they walk through how if you want to avoid the basal trichomy you need to root using the outgroup, not by designating the node (which they designate using 1:5 in the birdorders example).<br><br>- Does this help?.</div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><br><br>On Monday, August 14, 2017 at 5:39:42 PM UTC+2, Guy Leonard wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Considering the attached tree in &quot;rooting_issue.png&quot;; in the first panel the tree is unrooted, in the second the tree is rooted on node 14. <div><br></div><div>Why then does the topology collapse to what looks like a polytomy? I don&#39;t think it actually is, as if you zoom in it definitely looks like there are two vertical lines drawn around node 11 in the second panel - is there a way that I have missed to expand the root here? It feels like something I have read but no amount of searching is bringing up something that I can alter...</div><div><br></div><div>I have attached what I think it should look like in &quot;how it should look&quot; png.</div><div><br></div><div>Thanks for any help!</div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ]
   ]
