     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Ben, And thanks again!.<div>Here&#39;s my issue in code. Although, this may be somewhat more of an ape issue (and probably really just my misunderstanding) than a ggtree issue. Trees 1-6 end up with a trichomy (which just isn&#39;t desirable to see), but tree 7 which I opened up externally in NJPlot (etc) and rerooted and then plotted shows the desired visual output. I do really want to avoid having to open each tree first if I can just specific a known node or taxa to root on but there we go...<div><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#008\">set</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">seed</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1234</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>tree1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> rtree</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">10</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>tree_one </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> ggtree</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">tree1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> geom_tiplab</span><span style=\"color:#660\">()</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> geom_text2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">aes</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">subset</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=!</span><span style=\"color:#000\">isTip</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> label</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">node</spa